[94mText:[0m The ferry left the harbor before the sun was fully up. Maria stood at the railing and watched the lamps on the quay go out one by one. She had not slept. Behind her, Tom was coiling the last of the wet ropes, slowly, the way he had done it every morning for thirty years. Neither of them spoke. The sea was flat and pale, and somewhere below deck a kettle had started to sing. It was going to be a long crossing, and both of them knew it.
